WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.339
People took what happened to George

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:03.959
Floyd and grafted that onto a

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.999
larger, broader narrative

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.229
to make the case that this is

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.270
actually endemic to American police.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:12.309
The statistics just aren't there.

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:14.469
If you look at things like deadly

00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:16.449
police, use of force is incredibly

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:18.219
rare occurrence.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:19.539
The killing of George Floyd and

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:21.639
Breonna Taylor in 2020 touched

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:23.559
off a summer of protests over police

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:25.629
brutality, especially toward

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:27.969
African-Americans and Hispanics.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:29.919
Too many the killings cemented as

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.839
fact a narrative that began with the

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:33.939
2014 death of Michael

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:35.799
Brown in Ferguson, Missouri, and

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:37.509
has found expression in highly

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:39.369
charged slogans such as All

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:41.619
Cops are Bastards and Defund

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:43.449
the Police. Reformers charge

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:45.339
that cops, far from keeping the

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:47.289
peace, are simply the most visible

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:49.179
agents of white supremacy that

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:51.579
systematically surveil some punishes

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:53.619
racial and ethnic minorities.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:55.509
Long stalled reforms such

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:57.429
as abolishing qualified immunity

00:00:57.430 --> 00:00:59.409
and ending cash bail, made big

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:01.359
gains as massive crowds

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.129
marched under the banner of Black

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:04.208
Lives Matter.

00:01:04.209 --> 00:01:06.069
But what if the narrative that

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:07.989
police are increasingly dangerous,

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:09.849
violent and unaccountable

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:11.109
is wrong.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:12.110
In Criminal [In]Justice

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:14.589
Rafael A. Mangual argues that police

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:16.389
violence is in fact rare and

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:17.199
declining.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.819
He says that the criminal justice

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:20.919
reforms favored by social justice

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:23.049
activists and many libertarians

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:25.149
will make life worse for communities

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:25.779
of color.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:27.339
If we're going to have an honest

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:29.229
conversation about where reform

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:30.909
needs to happen, we have to be

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:32.619
realistic about what the real scope

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:34.299
of the problem is, because that's

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:35.259
the best way that we're going to be

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.179
able to assess what can actually

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:38.469
fix that problem, what we can

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:40.119
reasonably expect to be the results

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:41.409
from those efforts.

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:42.489
Mangual works at the Manhattan

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:44.349
Institute, the New York City think

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:46.239
tank that played a foundational role

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:48.039
in the shift in policing tactics

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:50.169
that began in the early 1990s.

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:51.699
The Manhattan Institute published

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:53.229
the work of George L Kelling and

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:55.089
James Q Wilson on broken windows

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:56.619
policing and championed the

00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:58.239
development of the crime tracking

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:00.189
program CompStat under New York

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:01.749
City Police Commissioner William

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:02.889
Bratton.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:04.089
Mangual grew up in Brooklyn in Long

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:06.129
Island, the half Dominican half

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:07.839
Puerto Rican son of a New York

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:09.698
Police Department detective.

00:02:09.699 --> 00:02:11.169
He attended Baruch College in the

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:12.879
City University of New York system

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:14.109
and holds a law degree from

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:16.479
Chicago's DePaul University.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:17.529
Reason talked with him about the

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.539
facts and rhetoric surrounding law

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.309
enforcement, whether violent crime

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:23.139
is actually rising, and what are

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:25.599
the best ways to keep the peace

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:27.459
without harassing and locking

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:28.749
up innocent people.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:36.479
Rafael Mangual, thanks for talking

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:36.899
to Reason.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:37.949
Thanks so much for having me.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:39.869
What is the elevator pitch for

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:40.870
Criminal [In]Justice?

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:44.039
I guess the best the basic elevator

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:45.989
pitch is the the book

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:47.759
interrogates the dominant narratives

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:48.899
that have been driving our criminal

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.129
justice reform debate in this

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:51.359
country.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.249
It explores to what degree

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.109
is the accusation that

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:56.819
America has an overincarceration

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.499
problem, a police violence problem

00:02:58.500 --> 00:02:59.669
overstated?

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:01.769
And how does that overstatement

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:03.749
lead to policies that perhaps

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.219
are misguided? What's the downside

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:07.409
risk associated with that policy

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:08.369
agenda, and who does it

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:09.749
disproportionately fall on?

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:11.249
Well, the reason I asked that last

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.079
question is because the

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:14.699
narratives and the debate about

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:16.559
criminal justice reform in the

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:18.599
U.S. has been largely

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.459
driven by concerns for

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.499
discrete communities that

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:23.999
bear the brunt of the costs

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.649
associated with the kind of robust

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:26.879
enforcement programs that have been

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:27.899
criticized.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:29.699
So, I mean, you're talking about

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:31.919
black and Hispanic or Latino

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:32.909
populations.

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:34.499
And mostly young male, black,

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:35.500
Hispanic.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:37.739
You know, I guess why don't we start

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:38.969
with George Floyd, right?

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:40.949
Because that's the,

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:42.209
you know, the immediate trigger

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:44.699
point of the past couple of years.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:46.559
What you

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.839
know, what what is wrong about

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:50.609
the narrative coming out of George

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:52.619
Floyd's death at the hands

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:53.729
of police?

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:55.379
Yeah. You know, what are the main

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:57.419
strains that are like, okay,

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:59.699
this is wrong because, I mean, we

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:02.069
we all saw what we saw, which is

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:04.109
horror cops indifferently,

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:06.269
kind of killing a guy who was

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:06.929
subdued.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:08.489
Yeah. It was a power trip, right?

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:09.779
I mean, it was. It was an absolute

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.939
power trip. It was something that I

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:13.829
have not yet spoken to

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:15.989
a police officer who watched

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:17.398
that video and wasn't equally

00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:18.389
horrified.

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:19.919
I think the biggest thing that that

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:21.778
the narrative about that incident

00:04:21.779 --> 00:04:23.729
gets wrong is that

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:25.619
it is largely characteristic

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:27.629
of policing in the United States.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:29.609
People took what happened to George

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:31.259
Floyd as they have taken other

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.179
controversial police uses of force

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:35.249
and grafted it, grafted that onto

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.409
a larger, broader narrative

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:38.639
to make the case that this is

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.319
actually endemic to American

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:41.369
policing. And that couldn't be

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:42.779
further from the truth.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:44.099
The statistics just aren't there.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.259
If you look at things like deadly

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:47.369
police use of force, it is

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:49.739
incredibly rare occurrence.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:51.689
It happens every day in

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:53.099
this country, which makes it sound

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:54.359
like it happens a lot.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.099
But you have to contextualize that

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:56.999
reality.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.949
Can you put. Yeah, put some numbers

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:00.059
on that. Yeah.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:02.189
You know, what is what is the amount

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:04.109
of police violence versus

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:05.789
the amount of interactions with

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:07.679
police? And is the rate

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:09.419
going up or going down or staying

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:09.869
the same?

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:11.099
It's gone down significantly.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:13.469
I mean, you know, just using NYPD

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:15.419
as as an example, in 1971,

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:16.799
which is the first year that the

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:18.419
department started keeping track of

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:19.829
their use of force statistics, they

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:21.959
shot more than 220 people.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:23.369
That number is down to, like, a

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:24.749
dozen.

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:26.329
So significant progress with respect

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:27.330
to that.

